WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.800
So today on our deep dive, we're actually dismantling probably the most universal but

00:07.800 --> 00:11.820
invisible piece of technology that's, you know, sitting right in front of you right now.

00:11.900 --> 00:12.660
Right. The keyboard.

00:12.860 --> 00:18.140
Yeah, the keyboard. We're getting into a really fascinating paper from 2026 by an independent

00:18.140 --> 00:24.040
researcher named Fliction. It's titled The Eight-Litter Keyboard, Finger Channel Invariance,

00:24.260 --> 00:26.280
and the Motor Grammar of Text Entry.

00:26.400 --> 00:28.200
Which is quite a mouthful, but yeah.

00:28.200 --> 00:33.440
It is, yeah. But our mission today is basically to discover what your hands actually know when you

00:33.440 --> 00:40.980
sit down to type and how separating that bodily knowledge from the actual plastic grid could

00:40.980 --> 00:43.220
totally revolutionize how we interact with machines.

00:43.400 --> 00:44.200
Exactly, yeah.

00:44.640 --> 00:49.600
But to, I guess, to really understand the scale of what this paper is arguing, I want you to kind

00:49.600 --> 00:54.160
of imagine your daily commute. If I ask you to sit down right now with a blank piece of paper

00:54.160 --> 00:59.900
paper and draw a map of that drive from memory, every single turn, every stop sign, yield,

01:00.000 --> 01:02.800
whatever, there's a very high probability you'd fail.

01:02.880 --> 01:03.340
Oh, absolutely.

01:03.680 --> 01:08.060
Right. You'd forget a side street or put a traffic light where there's only a crosswalk.

01:08.220 --> 01:15.260
Yeah, because consciously recalling spatial data in a vacuum is incredibly difficult.

01:15.260 --> 01:22.700
Without the context of the physical environment, your brain just, it struggles to pull up the

01:22.700 --> 01:27.540
exact visual coordinates. You know, you'd start second guessing yourself before you even drew

01:27.540 --> 01:28.380
the second intersection.

01:28.540 --> 01:32.240
Exactly. But if you get in your car, your body just knows the way.

01:32.620 --> 01:37.600
You can drive that route while thinking about dinner or listening to a deep dive like this one.

01:37.780 --> 01:43.420
We accept that about driving and we accept that about like walking to the kitchen in the pitch dark.

01:43.420 --> 01:44.420
Yeah. Muscle memory.

01:44.580 --> 01:48.480
Right. Yet we have this profound contradiction when it comes to typing.

01:49.620 --> 01:54.420
Why is it that I can type 80 words a minute completely effortlessly, but if you had me a blank

01:54.420 --> 01:59.220
grid of rectangles, I couldn't accurately fill in the QWERTY layout.

01:59.440 --> 01:59.920
It's true.

02:00.060 --> 02:04.760
Like I honestly couldn't tell you what letter sits directly below the G, yet my hands type G and

02:04.760 --> 02:06.920
then move to whatever's next flawlessly.

02:07.280 --> 02:13.180
Well, we operate under this massive kind of unexamined assumption that typing is fundamentally a visual

02:13.180 --> 02:18.760
task that we've just memorized. We assume we're interacting with a static map of 40 isolated

02:18.760 --> 02:24.120
symbols. And that being a fast typist just means you have a perfect mental photograph of that grid.

02:24.340 --> 02:25.880
Which we clearly don't.

02:25.960 --> 02:31.600
Right. We don't. So what Flicksheen argues in this paper is that typing isn't a visual map at all.

02:31.820 --> 02:32.720
It's a motor grammar.

02:32.960 --> 02:33.520
Motor grammar.

02:33.520 --> 02:40.240
Yeah. The body actually arrives at the keyboard with a complex internal division of labor already

02:40.240 --> 02:44.760
installed in the fingers. And it's completely independent of those plastic squares.

02:45.140 --> 02:49.220
A motor grammar. I mean, I love that phrasing, but the title of the paper is the eight letter

02:49.220 --> 02:55.060
keyboard. And my immediate thought looking at the source material was, well, English has 26 letters.

02:55.160 --> 02:58.160
So what are we doing here? Are we inventing a new language?

02:58.160 --> 03:03.000
Right, right. It's a deliberately provocative title. And the author admits it's a bit of a

03:03.000 --> 03:04.220
Trojan horse, honestly.

03:04.460 --> 03:04.740
Okay.

03:04.900 --> 03:09.380
It doesn't mean the English language is being reduced to eight characters. What it means is that

03:09.380 --> 03:14.880
our eight non-thumb fingers act as the primary addressing structure for the entire system.

03:15.020 --> 03:15.860
Oh, I see.

03:16.040 --> 03:19.900
Yeah. Those eight fingers are the fundamental letters of an embodied alphabet.

03:20.340 --> 03:25.780
So instead of thinking I need to press the Q key, the underlying system in my brain is actually

03:25.780 --> 03:30.820
built around the command. I need to activate my left little finger.

03:31.260 --> 03:36.240
Precisely. If you break down the channels, it's astonishing how strictly territorial our hands are

03:36.240 --> 03:37.340
on a QWERTY layout.

03:37.520 --> 03:38.460
Okay. How so?

03:38.820 --> 03:44.280
Well, think about the feeling of typing. The left little finger owns a very specific column,

03:44.420 --> 03:45.220
Q, A, and Z.

03:45.500 --> 03:45.760
Right.

03:45.900 --> 03:51.420
And the left ring finger owns W, S, and X. The left middle finger owns E, D, and C.

03:51.840 --> 03:53.940
And this pattern just continues across the hands.

03:54.200 --> 03:54.400
Yeah.

03:54.400 --> 03:59.860
You aren't reaching for coordinates on an X, Y axis. You're activating specific finger

03:59.860 --> 04:04.520
channels and then giving them a secondary instruction to either reach up, stay neutral,

04:04.700 --> 04:05.320
or reach down.

04:05.500 --> 04:10.160
Okay. So let's unpack this because I'm visualizing my hand right now and my index fingers do way

04:10.160 --> 04:14.920
more work than just one column. Like if the rule is strict finger channels, the index fingers

04:14.920 --> 04:16.740
seem to break that rule immediately.

04:17.000 --> 04:21.880
They do. And the paper specifically addresses this exception. Fliction calls it the index fork.

04:21.920 --> 04:22.660
The index fork.

04:22.660 --> 04:29.180
Yeah. The index fingers don't just own a single vertical column like the ring or pinky fingers.

04:29.620 --> 04:33.640
Because of the anatomy of the human hand and, you know, the width of the keyboard,

04:33.860 --> 04:35.540
they branch out laterally.

04:35.700 --> 04:35.980
Okay.

04:35.980 --> 04:42.760
So your left index finger owns R, F, knee, but it also has the column next to it, T, G, and B.

04:43.120 --> 04:48.500
And the right index finger mirrors this. It owns U, J, M, and then Y, H, N.

04:48.700 --> 04:54.380
That makes sense. That accounts for all the letters, but what about the thumbs? Do they count as part of this eight-letter grammar?

04:54.380 --> 05:01.060
The thumbs are categorized as shared or peripheral mode channels. They don't actually belong to the primary letter map.

05:01.800 --> 05:09.520
In ordinary typing, they usually share the spacebar duty, or like if you use a split ergonomic keyboard, they might handle layer or editing keys.

05:09.660 --> 05:10.080
Got it.

05:10.080 --> 05:17.820
Their job is compositional. They alter or time the output of the eight primary channels rather than generating the core letters themselves.

05:17.820 --> 05:22.860
So to really drive this home, the paper features this incredible hand-drawn artifact,

05:23.420 --> 05:28.180
and instead of drawing a normal keyboard with all those little squares and rectangles, the author just draws two hands.

05:28.300 --> 05:29.220
Yeah, it's a great visual.

05:29.580 --> 05:34.420
It really is. But the fingers are stretched out, like elongated into these territories.

05:34.760 --> 05:41.720
So inside the drawing of the left ring finger, for example, the letters W, S, and X are written top to bottom.

05:41.720 --> 05:47.640
And it visually proves that to our bodies, these aren't rows stretching horizontally across a board.

05:47.800 --> 05:54.820
They're upward, neutral, and downward directional states, you know, within a specific finger's isolated channel.

05:54.920 --> 05:59.940
Exactly. The visual representation completely reorients how you think about the space.

06:00.240 --> 06:03.460
But the most crucial part of that drawing is actually the heading.

06:03.740 --> 06:04.340
Oh, right.

06:04.340 --> 06:15.480
The title of the illustration is just a string of symbols, exclamation point, at symbol, hash, dollar sign, ampersand, asterisk, open parenthesis, close parenthesis.

06:15.700 --> 06:18.580
Right. I stared at that for a solid minute before it clicked.

06:18.860 --> 06:22.120
It's kind of cryptic. Let's break down why that string of symbols is there.

06:22.460 --> 06:27.980
In computing, a checksum is a small block of data used to verify that a much larger file hasn't been corrupted.

06:28.140 --> 06:28.420
Okay.

06:28.820 --> 06:32.720
Fliction uses this heading as a checksum for the brain's internal system architecture.

06:32.840 --> 06:33.180
Wow.

06:33.180 --> 06:39.720
If you look at a standard QWERTY keyboard, those are the shifted symbols for the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4.

06:39.980 --> 06:44.140
And then it explicitly skips 5 and 6 and jumps to 7, 8, 9, and 0.

06:44.280 --> 06:48.740
Because the 5 and the 6 belong to the inward forks of the index fingers.

06:48.880 --> 06:49.080
Yes.

06:49.180 --> 06:51.880
They aren't the primary, neutral, resting columns.

06:52.060 --> 06:52.720
That's it, exactly.

06:53.120 --> 07:00.400
Those eight shifted number symbols represent the pure primary digits assigned to the eight fingers when they aren't branching out into the fork.

07:00.500 --> 07:01.580
That is so clever.

07:01.860 --> 07:02.040
Right.

07:02.040 --> 07:05.160
It's a cryptic but perfect summary of the motor grammar.

07:05.800 --> 07:12.100
Eight primary channels, two index extensions in the middle, and unbounded space on the periphery for the thumbs.

07:12.100 --> 07:24.140
So, if this is true, if typing is really about activating finger channels and directional states rather than hitting a specific physical coordinate, how does this change the way we look at typing mistakes?

07:24.380 --> 07:25.800
That's the real test of the theory.

07:25.940 --> 07:28.920
Because I always assume that when I make a typo, I'm just clumsy.

07:28.920 --> 07:33.420
Like, I physically missed the target because my hand-eye coordination failed for a millisecond.

07:33.580 --> 07:33.800
Right.

07:33.800 --> 07:40.440
But if we treat typing as a motor grammar, then errors shouldn't be random physical misses.

07:40.860 --> 07:42.340
They should follow grammatical rules.

07:42.860 --> 07:46.320
And the paper's error predictions back this up perfectly.

07:46.400 --> 07:46.700
Really?

07:46.700 --> 07:47.040
Yeah.

07:47.040 --> 07:52.940
When you make a typo, you will often preserve the finger identity while messing up the directional state.

07:53.760 --> 07:55.840
For example, say you meant to type an E.

07:56.460 --> 08:02.160
The correct grammatical command is activate left middle finger directional state upward.

08:02.160 --> 08:04.000
But instead, you type a D.

08:04.360 --> 08:08.400
Which is the exact same left middle finger, just in the neutral home state.

08:08.600 --> 08:09.040
Exactly.

08:09.280 --> 08:12.300
So, my brain got the channel right, but failed on the elevation.

08:12.660 --> 08:13.100
Precisely.

08:13.580 --> 08:21.800
Or conversely, you might preserve the directional state, say the upward reach, but accidentally fire the adjacent finger channel, typing an R instead of an E.

08:21.980 --> 08:23.080
Oh, I do that all the time.

08:23.140 --> 08:23.360
Right.

08:23.860 --> 08:30.020
And this kind of error matrix is profound because it proves that typing is a conditional generative system in the brain.

08:30.020 --> 08:35.760
It's not just a rigid sequence of muscular contractions, which is, you know, the phrase muscle memory implies.

08:36.060 --> 08:36.120
Right.

08:36.240 --> 08:38.880
It's a structured grammatical language your hands are speaking.

08:39.220 --> 08:41.280
So, here's where it gets really interesting to me.

08:41.740 --> 08:51.340
If our bodies intuitively want this eight-channel directional system, I don't understand why the keyboard in front of me looks the way it does.

08:51.360 --> 08:52.340
Yeah, the QWERTY layout.

08:52.500 --> 08:55.640
Like, who forced us into this staggered plastic grid?

08:55.640 --> 09:02.880
If it's not actually aligned with our internal model of the task, how did it become the universal standard?

09:03.300 --> 09:10.060
Well, to answer that, the source material requires us to look at the machines inventors were stealing from in the 1850s.

09:10.440 --> 09:16.620
We have to look at the Victorian era, where the distinction between playing a musical instrument and typing text was almost non-existent.

09:16.760 --> 09:17.480
Wow, really?

09:17.680 --> 09:17.840
Yeah.

09:17.920 --> 09:19.740
They occupied a shared design space.

09:19.740 --> 09:21.500
That is so hard to wrap my head around.

09:21.660 --> 09:25.880
Like, a piano and a typewriter seem like completely different categories of objects.

09:25.980 --> 09:26.900
They do to us now.

09:27.020 --> 09:27.240
Yeah.

09:27.620 --> 09:32.280
But the concept of a key back then didn't mean a plastic square with a letter printed on it.

09:32.660 --> 09:36.740
A key was just a general mechanical proposition for selecting a discrete event.

09:36.860 --> 09:37.360
Oh, interesting.

09:37.360 --> 09:44.020
And musical instruments, specifically the piano and the harpsichord, had spent centuries perfecting the engineering of keys.

09:44.620 --> 09:46.980
Piano makers had figured out the optimal spacing.

09:47.460 --> 09:55.180
There were turn springs, the leverage, and, you know, the ergonomic training required for skilled human fingers to operate them rapidly without fatigue.

09:55.440 --> 10:02.940
So Victorian inventors looking at a piano and seeing a typewriter is like us looking at a smartphone touchscreen today and seeing a steering wheel.

10:03.400 --> 10:06.480
Like, the hardware was just a blank canvas for human output.

10:06.480 --> 10:07.920
That is a great analogy.

10:08.420 --> 10:08.660
Literally.

10:09.120 --> 10:12.480
The paper brings up David Edward Hughes' printing telegraph from the 1850s.

10:13.260 --> 10:13.480
Okay.

10:13.920 --> 10:15.360
This wasn't a grid of letters.

10:15.880 --> 10:20.420
It used a piano-like keyboard with 14 white keys and 14 black keys.

10:20.460 --> 10:22.980
A 28-key piano for sending telegraphs.

10:23.060 --> 10:26.260
So how did they know what letter they were sending if there were no letters on the keys?

10:26.460 --> 10:27.880
Pitch was completely irrelevant.

10:28.580 --> 10:31.120
Pressing a key generated an electrical pulse.

10:31.120 --> 10:38.980
The timing of that pulse coordinated with the continuously rotating character mechanism on the receiving end to print a specific letter.

10:39.200 --> 10:39.740
Wait, really?

10:39.960 --> 10:40.160
Yeah.

10:40.880 --> 10:44.940
So if you press the C key at exactly the right millisecond, the receiver printed an A.

10:45.380 --> 10:48.480
What mattered was rhythmic touch, precision, and timing.

10:49.260 --> 10:52.580
The musical surface was completely co-opted for telecommunications.

10:52.900 --> 10:53.540
That's incredible.

10:53.540 --> 10:59.900
And then there's Giuseppe Ravitsa's machine, the Cimbalo Scrivano, which translates to writing harpsichord.

11:00.080 --> 11:00.340
Yes.

11:00.860 --> 11:05.300
This was a direct writing machine, essentially an early prototype of the typewriter.

11:05.940 --> 11:13.400
It had 31 ivory and mother-of-pearl keys arranged in two superposed lines, exactly like a harpsichord manual.

11:13.800 --> 11:14.120
Wow.

11:14.340 --> 11:16.780
The inventor wasn't trying to hide the genealogy of the machine.

11:17.100 --> 11:18.260
He was leaning into it.

11:18.760 --> 11:21.740
Writing was being mechanized through the physical vocabulary of music.

11:21.740 --> 11:27.220
Okay, but we had these beautiful ergonomic musical writing machines that worked seamlessly with our hands.

11:27.640 --> 11:32.060
Why did we abandon the writing harpsichord and end up with the QWERTY grid we have today?

11:32.420 --> 11:34.320
It really came down to industrial pragmatism.

11:34.420 --> 11:39.700
The compact typewriter grid won out for reasons that had nothing to do with human motor efficiency.

11:39.960 --> 11:40.380
Like what?

11:40.540 --> 11:47.800
Well, it won because of portability, ease of manufacturing, the fact that it took up less space on a crowded desk,

11:47.800 --> 11:53.360
and crucially, it allowed for direct visual search by absolute novices.

11:53.540 --> 11:53.980
Oh, right.

11:54.100 --> 11:55.200
The hunt-and-peck method.

11:55.420 --> 12:04.760
If you don't know how to type, a compact grid with letters explicitly printed on it is much less intimidating than a two-tiered ivory harpsichord where you have to learn a motor grammar first.

12:04.820 --> 12:05.180
Exactly.

12:05.180 --> 12:10.300
But there was a massive consequence to the visual grid winning the commercial war.

12:10.480 --> 12:10.980
Which was?

12:11.360 --> 12:18.920
Once every symbol was assigned a visible, dedicated cap, the key cap itself became viewed as the fundamental unit of competence.

12:19.120 --> 12:19.920
Oh, I see.

12:20.220 --> 12:26.200
The actual bodily division of labor, the fact that your left ring finger owns W, S, and X,

12:26.320 --> 12:26.540
Yeah.

12:26.640 --> 12:27.460
became an afterthought.

12:27.680 --> 12:32.720
It was just a proper technique you might learn in a typing class after the device was already designed.

12:32.720 --> 12:36.440
The visual grid completely obscured the underlying motor grammar.

12:36.860 --> 12:40.620
So we locked ourselves into a piece of hardware that caters to beginners hunting for letters,

12:40.860 --> 12:45.020
and basically ignores how our brains actually want to organize the information once we're skilled.

12:45.400 --> 12:46.740
That's the tragedy of it, yeah.

12:47.620 --> 12:51.480
But Flexian, the author of this paper, didn't just write a historical critique about this.

12:51.780 --> 12:59.160
They actually built working prototypes to strip the visual grid away and prove that the motor grammar exists independently.

12:59.480 --> 12:59.720
Right.

12:59.880 --> 13:00.960
Using the piano again.

13:01.140 --> 13:01.560
Exactly.

13:01.560 --> 13:05.220
And to do it, they used a modern 88-key MIDI controller.

13:05.420 --> 13:07.120
They went back to the Victorian era.

13:07.620 --> 13:10.720
Okay, so the first prototype is what they call the unfolded ribbon.

13:11.520 --> 13:16.320
Imagine taking your standard computer keyboard, cutting it horizontally into row segments,

13:16.680 --> 13:21.380
and stretching those segments end-to-end across all 88 keys of a piano.

13:21.580 --> 13:21.780
Right.

13:21.860 --> 13:25.720
It exaggerates the layout to reveal the physical distance hidden in a normal keyboard.

13:25.860 --> 13:26.400
How so?

13:26.400 --> 13:31.180
Well, on a standard chordy board, if you need to hit the control key in a letter simultaneously,

13:31.180 --> 13:33.940
it's a small chord-like span for your hand.

13:34.140 --> 13:34.440
Right.

13:34.440 --> 13:40.380
But on this unfolded ribbon across a piano, moving from a modifier key all the way down to a letter,

13:40.840 --> 13:44.080
becomes a massive, sweeping instrumental gesture.

13:44.080 --> 13:46.400
Your arms are literally crossing the keyboard.

13:46.400 --> 13:52.380
The paper mentions that the raised black keys on the piano serve literally as an upper layer for functions,

13:52.540 --> 13:54.040
which is just wonderfully literal.

13:54.040 --> 13:59.260
It translates the metaphorical shift state of a computer into a physical elevation.

14:00.060 --> 14:04.960
But as the author notes, this unfolded ribbon isn't meant to be a practical daily driver.

14:05.040 --> 14:05.700
Oh, for sure.

14:05.740 --> 14:06.740
It's demonstrative.

14:06.880 --> 14:07.060
Right.

14:07.320 --> 14:12.420
It proves that you can topologically unfold the keyboard, destroying the visual grid,

14:12.640 --> 14:15.220
without losing the relational structure of the symbols.

14:15.560 --> 14:19.740
Okay, but the second prototype is where my brain really started doing backflips.

14:19.740 --> 14:25.540
It's called the compressed scale degree mapping, or the C-sharp minor experiment.

14:26.220 --> 14:31.620
Walk me through this, because compressing a keyboard onto a few piano notes sounds physically impossible.

14:31.900 --> 14:36.360
It is a mind-bending concept that relies entirely on the motor grammar we established earlier.

14:37.080 --> 14:40.520
Instead of stretching the keyboard out, this prototype compresses it drastically.

14:40.760 --> 14:40.980
Okay.

14:41.200 --> 14:45.800
It assigns the chordy columns to a repeated five-note pitch pattern in each hand.

14:46.120 --> 14:48.120
So let's take the scale of C-sharp minor.

14:48.380 --> 14:48.620
Right.

14:48.620 --> 14:50.940
Your left hand sits on five consecutive notes.

14:51.340 --> 14:53.580
The C-sharp key is assigned to your little finger.

14:53.800 --> 14:57.380
Because we know the little finger's channel owns Q, A, and Z,

14:57.700 --> 15:02.560
that single C-sharp piano key now represents all three of those letters.

15:02.980 --> 15:03.820
Stop right there.

15:04.060 --> 15:04.860
This is where I struggle.

15:05.240 --> 15:08.240
My left pinky presses the C-sharp key on the piano.

15:08.240 --> 15:14.600
How does the computer know, I mean, the letter Q, the letter A, or the letter Z?

15:15.100 --> 15:21.120
Like, if all three are mapped to one physical key, haven't we just created a massive traffic jam of letters?

15:21.120 --> 15:23.560
That is the crucial hurdle of the experiment.

15:24.440 --> 15:28.880
By mapping an entire column to a single piano note, you preserve the finger ownership.

15:29.380 --> 15:31.600
Your pinky is still doing the work it's trained to do.

15:31.680 --> 15:31.880
Right.

15:32.000 --> 15:34.500
But you have completely collapsed the directional parameter.

15:34.940 --> 15:41.140
The system knows which finger fired, but it no longer knows if you intended to reach up, stay home, or reach down.

15:41.260 --> 15:44.300
Because the piano key doesn't move up or down, it just presses inward.

15:44.840 --> 15:45.660
So how do you fix it?

15:45.680 --> 15:50.860
Because if you can't tell the system which of the three letters you want, this is just a useless art project, not a functioning keyboard.

15:51.180 --> 15:55.020
Well, the paper outlines several mechanisms to recover that missing directional state,

15:55.160 --> 15:58.160
and each choice changes the physical demands on the user.

15:58.220 --> 15:59.040
Okay, what are the options?

15:59.040 --> 16:03.520
One option is to use the thumb to hold a separate modifier key that selects the row.

16:04.420 --> 16:10.760
So hold thumb key A to mean upper row, thumb key B to mean lower row.

16:10.760 --> 16:16.160
Like holding a shift key, but instead of capitalizing a letter, it just tells the computer I'm aiming for the top row.

16:16.340 --> 16:16.540
Right.

16:16.920 --> 16:20.560
Another option is note velocity, which is how hard you strike the piano key.

16:20.780 --> 16:21.360
Oh boy.

16:21.460 --> 16:21.620
Yeah.

16:22.000 --> 16:27.240
A soft strike could mean the lower row, a medium strike the home row, and a hard strike the upper row.

16:27.500 --> 16:28.880
I would reject that immediately.

16:29.340 --> 16:30.700
Note velocity is continuous.

16:31.480 --> 16:36.200
Trying to reliably hit a key with medium force at 80 words a minute sounds like a nightmare.

16:36.520 --> 16:38.200
I'd be typing completely random rows.

16:38.200 --> 16:43.400
And the author agrees. Velocity is very difficult for humans to reproduce categorically at high speeds.

16:43.540 --> 16:43.780
Yeah.

16:44.180 --> 16:49.620
You could also use foot pedals to switch rows, which preserves the hands but requires a whole new physical coordination.

16:50.060 --> 16:55.360
But the most musically fascinating option the prototype explores is using chord voicings.

16:55.800 --> 16:56.780
Okay, wait, let me stop you there.

16:56.780 --> 17:02.480
If I'm typing the word cat, not only do my fingers have to find the C, A, and T channels,

17:02.840 --> 17:09.560
but do I have to concurrently compose a three-note chord for every single letter just to tell the computer what row I mean?

17:09.900 --> 17:11.500
This sounds wild, I know.

17:11.540 --> 17:15.240
Doesn't that require a master's degree in music theory just to send an email?

17:15.240 --> 17:19.280
It sounds incredibly complex, but the mechanism is actually quite elegant.

17:19.740 --> 17:23.120
The user isn't inventing complex jazz harmony on the fly.

17:23.760 --> 17:28.640
You are striking three keys at once, a triad shape that is comfortable for the hand.

17:28.740 --> 17:29.020
Okay.

17:29.440 --> 17:36.160
The system is programmed so that the highest note in that triad corresponds to the finger channel you want to activate.

17:36.160 --> 17:41.180
So if my pinky plays the highest note, the system knows I'm activating the P-A-Z channel.

17:41.220 --> 17:41.640
Exactly.

17:42.480 --> 17:48.240
And then the shape of the rest of the chord, whether the lower and middle notes are spaced closely together or spread apart,

17:48.640 --> 17:51.100
gives a soft probability to the chordy row.

17:51.220 --> 17:51.660
Oh, wow.

17:51.820 --> 17:56.720
The lower, middle, and upper structures of that triad correspond to the lower, home, and upper rows.

17:56.720 --> 18:02.140
So by playing a specific practice triad shape where your pinky is the melody note,

18:02.380 --> 18:08.560
you are giving the system the probability data it needs to select the Q instead of the Z.

18:08.840 --> 18:09.820
That is wild.

18:09.920 --> 18:12.160
It's like hiding text inside a symphony.

18:12.620 --> 18:13.320
It really is.

18:13.400 --> 18:16.720
The paper actually talks about this making text entries sound like music.

18:17.380 --> 18:20.280
Because the motor skeleton dictates the letters you are typing,

18:20.740 --> 18:24.280
an independent software layer can reharmonize those inputs.

18:24.460 --> 18:24.680
Yeah.

18:24.680 --> 18:27.880
So I could be sitting there typing out a boring financial spreadsheet,

18:28.160 --> 18:32.000
but to anyone listening in the room, it sounds like I'm playing a cohesive pop song.

18:32.080 --> 18:32.480
Exactly.

18:33.320 --> 18:37.040
The system separates the symbolic validity from the musical rendering.

18:37.380 --> 18:37.680
Oh.

18:37.880 --> 18:41.020
The sequence of your fingers provides the hard constraint, the letters,

18:41.580 --> 18:47.060
while the musical layer determines the pitch center, the octave, and the accompaniment to make it sound good.

18:47.680 --> 18:50.900
The computer doesn't need to know the song to decode your text,

18:51.120 --> 18:53.700
and the listener doesn't need to know the text to enjoy the song.

18:53.700 --> 18:55.680
So, what does this all mean?

18:55.840 --> 18:59.180
I mean, I'm looking at this MIDI piano setup, and my head is spinning.

18:59.780 --> 19:03.820
One piano key representing three different letters based on chord probability.

19:04.220 --> 19:09.500
It seems incredibly ambiguous and complicated compared to just pressing a plastic button with a G on it.

19:09.880 --> 19:10.720
It is ambiguous.

19:11.120 --> 19:17.880
But what Fliction points out is that this level of ambiguity is perfectly analogous to how modern smartphones already operate.

19:17.980 --> 19:18.720
Wait, really?

19:18.720 --> 19:19.800
This isn't science fiction.

19:19.980 --> 19:26.620
You're already dealing with massive symbolic ambiguity every single time you use swipe typing on your phone screen.

19:26.940 --> 19:28.640
I never thought about it like that, but you're right.

19:28.900 --> 19:33.640
When I swipe a word, my finger is just dragging a messy, imprecise line across the glass.

19:33.900 --> 19:36.220
It's passing through or near dozens of letters.

19:37.000 --> 19:40.120
The raw physical gesture itself doesn't actually spell the word.

19:40.360 --> 19:42.520
The raw gesture underdetermines the symbol.

19:42.520 --> 19:50.580
When you swipe through Q, W, E, A, S, the phone doesn't know where the intended letters end and the incidental crossings begin.

19:50.720 --> 19:50.960
Right.

19:51.100 --> 19:54.560
What happens is that a swipe decoder receives a candidate set.

19:54.880 --> 19:59.580
It says, okay, based on this smudge, this segment of the gesture probably means W, S, or X.

19:59.580 --> 20:02.720
Let's pause on a term the paper uses here, equivalence class.

20:03.400 --> 20:13.140
So when we say equivalence class, we just mean the computer is looking at a swipe or a piano key, and instead of seeing one definitive answer, it just sees a bucket of equal possibilities.

20:13.480 --> 20:14.800
That is the perfect definition.

20:14.800 --> 20:19.080
The machine gets a menu of options based on the finger's general location.

20:19.940 --> 20:26.780
And to handle this ambiguity, the pipeline of text entry has to be strictly separated into four distinct operations.

20:27.460 --> 20:30.780
Record, recognize, admit, and commit.

20:31.260 --> 20:33.340
Record, recognize, admit, commit.

20:33.440 --> 20:34.400
Okay, let's ground this.

20:34.700 --> 20:39.920
It's like, imagine we're in an incredibly loud, crowded restaurant, and I mumble half a sentence across the table to you.

20:40.120 --> 20:40.280
Okay.

20:40.540 --> 20:43.020
The raw sound hitting your eardrum, that's the record step.

20:43.080 --> 20:44.040
It's a mess of audio.

20:44.260 --> 20:44.480
Right.

20:44.480 --> 20:47.280
But your brain recognizes the vowels and the cadence.

20:47.900 --> 20:53.020
Then you use the context of our ongoing conversation to admit a few possible phrases I might have said.

20:53.200 --> 20:57.320
And finally, your brain commits to the one phrase that makes the most logical sense in context.

20:57.900 --> 21:03.380
The actual disambiguation of my mumbled words is happening in your brain, not in my mouth.

21:03.620 --> 21:04.900
That is an excellent analogy.

21:05.260 --> 21:07.780
We are doing that exact same thing with our devices now.

21:08.300 --> 21:13.840
And if we connect this to the bigger picture, fliction is identifying the ultimate design variable in modern computing.

21:13.840 --> 21:16.280
The location of disambiguation.

21:16.280 --> 21:19.120
The location of disambiguation.

21:19.120 --> 21:19.380
Yeah.

21:19.380 --> 21:25.580
In the 1980s, with a clunky IBM mechanical keyboard, the physical hardware did the disambiguation.

21:26.140 --> 21:31.300
You pressed a discrete button, a specific circuit closed, and the machine knew exactly what you meant.

21:31.640 --> 21:34.160
The disambiguation was physically located in the surface.

21:34.160 --> 21:44.000
But today, with swipe typing or these experimental MIDI keyboards, the gesture merely provides the bucket of possibilities, the equivalence class.

21:44.000 --> 21:47.660
An algorithm downstream does the actual differentiating.

21:48.160 --> 21:51.600
The disambiguation has moved entirely from the hardware to the software.

21:51.760 --> 22:00.560
And that shift, moving the responsibility of figuring out what you mean from your own physical hand to a downstream algorithm, creates some massive implications for how we control our own words.

22:00.660 --> 22:00.960
How so?

22:01.380 --> 22:08.900
When the system relies on prediction and language models to figure out what you type from a bucket of possibilities, the input method suddenly becomes a language policy.

22:09.100 --> 22:10.100
A language policy.

22:10.380 --> 22:13.480
Meaning, machine gets a vote on what I'm actually allowed to say.

22:13.480 --> 22:21.020
If you were typing standard, predictable prose, like, see you at 5 p.m., a predictive decoder is a miracle, it saves you time.

22:21.580 --> 22:24.060
But what happens if you were typing a rare family name?

22:24.820 --> 22:27.040
Or a complex, secure password?

22:27.700 --> 22:29.580
Or lines of Python code?

22:30.120 --> 22:33.420
Or some new local slang you and your friends just invented?

22:33.680 --> 22:34.980
The decoder fights you.

22:35.080 --> 22:37.560
I mean, we've all experienced this visceral frustration.

22:37.860 --> 22:42.720
I had a situation last week where I was trying to text a specific medical term to a family member.

22:42.720 --> 22:47.140
And the phone forcibly autocorrected it to a common, unrelated word.

22:47.260 --> 22:47.700
It's classic.

22:47.940 --> 22:52.780
I had to go back, delete it, carefully retype the medical term, and the phone autocorrected it again.

22:53.260 --> 22:54.200
It was infuriating.

22:54.300 --> 22:57.440
I was fighting my own device just to communicate a basic fact.

22:57.620 --> 23:01.520
You were fighting it because the decoder is acting as an invisible author.

23:01.860 --> 23:05.260
It is normalizing your output to match its training data.

23:05.260 --> 23:09.920
The equivalence class allows for rapid entry, but it assumes a strong prior.

23:10.260 --> 23:17.840
A strong prior, meaning the algorithm has a pre-existing statistical expectation of what I'm going to say based on billions of texts sent by other people.

23:18.240 --> 23:18.800
Exactly.

23:19.320 --> 23:20.740
The system has a bias.

23:21.200 --> 23:26.900
If your intended word falls outside that statistical prior, the system penalizes you.

23:27.300 --> 23:27.580
Right.

23:27.580 --> 23:30.000
The source material is quite stern about this danger.

23:30.780 --> 23:37.860
It warns that any system designed around ambiguity must allow the user to easily inspect and override the decoder.

23:38.640 --> 23:42.960
It cannot, as the paper says, silently convert probability into fact.

23:43.340 --> 23:45.600
Silently convert probability into fact.

23:45.680 --> 23:47.300
That is a chilling way to phrase it.

23:47.640 --> 23:54.080
If the machine thinks there is a 90% chance I meant to type cat instead of cyst, it shouldn't just erase cyst without my permission.

23:54.080 --> 23:54.440
Yeah.

23:54.640 --> 23:58.000
Especially if cyst is an internal acronym I use at my job every day.

23:58.180 --> 23:58.620
Exactly.

23:59.440 --> 24:06.300
The final commitment of a word to the screen requires a policy, and that policy must remain transparent and inspectable by the user.

24:07.000 --> 24:11.460
Literal mode must always be an easy option, or we lose ownership of our own vocabulary.

24:11.780 --> 24:14.800
It makes you reflect on the micro-compromises we make daily.

24:15.560 --> 24:22.660
How often are we forced to accept a machine's slight mistranslation of our intent simply because it's too tedious to go back and fight the autocorrect?

24:22.860 --> 24:23.360
Too often.

24:23.360 --> 24:27.240
We trade our authority for convenience every time we let the decoder win the argument.

24:27.740 --> 24:29.820
We do, and it's a dangerous habit to form.

24:30.540 --> 24:34.260
But the paper doesn't just leave us with a warning about algorithmic overreach.

24:34.620 --> 24:36.560
It opens a door to the future.

24:37.260 --> 24:39.120
We keep skirting around this idea.

24:39.560 --> 24:41.640
The paper calls it substrate independence.

24:42.340 --> 24:43.340
Substrate independence.

24:43.340 --> 24:55.100
Basically, the idea that because the invariant is the motor grammar, the knowledge our hands already possess, and not the physical hardware, this skill doesn't care about the physical material, the substrate it's performed on.

24:55.100 --> 25:00.500
So if we are untethered from the QWERTY grid, what does the future of typing actually look like?

25:00.500 --> 25:03.820
Sliction gives some incredible examples of where this could go.

25:04.320 --> 25:11.120
Imagine wearable sensors, like rings or wristbands, that simply recognize finger identity and directional motion.

25:11.200 --> 25:11.940
Oh, wow.

25:11.940 --> 25:15.900
You could tap a novel in the air while walking down the street just by moving your fingers.

25:16.360 --> 25:20.420
Or a flat tabletop with radial touch zones that adapt to wherever you place your hands.

25:21.000 --> 25:26.000
Or a split device that sits inside your jacket pockets using small pressure rockers for each finger.

25:26.000 --> 25:35.480
So I could be standing on a crowded subway with my hands in my coat pocket, subtly twitching my fingers, and writing a perfectly punctuated, complex email to my boss.

25:35.620 --> 25:35.840
Yeah.

25:36.060 --> 25:38.620
And the system isn't looking for a plastic key.

25:38.880 --> 25:45.520
It's just looking for the internal grammatical command of my left ring finger followed by my right index finger.

25:45.840 --> 25:47.280
The physical interface vanishes.

25:47.480 --> 25:48.340
That's wild.

25:48.660 --> 25:51.920
But this raises an important question, right, beyond just convenience.

25:51.920 --> 25:58.580
This substrate independence has profound implications for rehabilitation and accessibility.

25:59.000 --> 25:59.700
Okay, how so?

25:59.900 --> 26:09.340
Let's say a user suffers an injury that removes their ability to use their left index finger or severely reduces their range of motion on one side of their body.

26:09.600 --> 26:14.160
Under the old paradigm where the visual grid is king, that user is completely locked out.

26:14.160 --> 26:21.680
Like, they might have to buy a specialized, expensive, one-handed keyboard and spend months learning a completely new layout from scratch.

26:21.920 --> 26:26.520
But under the A-letter model, the system doesn't need to discard their whole layout.

26:27.040 --> 26:31.440
It just has to solve what the paper calls a constrained remapping problem.

26:31.580 --> 26:33.360
A constrained remapping problem.

26:33.680 --> 26:40.540
It simply reassigns the RFVTGB channels, the ones formerly owned by the injured index finger,

26:40.540 --> 26:47.400
to a foot pedal or a thumb rocker or eye-tracking software, while leaving the rest of the hands exactly as they were trained.

26:47.400 --> 26:47.960
Wow.

26:48.480 --> 26:54.260
You restore their ability to communicate by changing the absolute minimum number of variables necessary.

26:54.520 --> 26:58.500
You preserve the user's acquired motor grammar as much as physically possible.

26:58.740 --> 26:59.060
Yes.

26:59.260 --> 27:03.720
That changes the entire paradigm of how we design accessible technology.

27:03.720 --> 27:04.400
It does.

27:04.520 --> 27:09.340
It challenges us to reconsider where the intelligence in our technology actually lives.

27:10.220 --> 27:11.820
Is it baked into the static hardware?

27:12.240 --> 27:13.960
Is it hidden in the predictive software?

27:14.220 --> 27:16.020
Or is it embodied in the human being?

27:16.200 --> 27:17.920
It has been a massive journey today.

27:18.460 --> 27:22.060
We started by realizing we type with this invisible eight-channel grammar.

27:22.460 --> 27:26.400
We explored Victorian typing harpsichords to figure out how we got trapped in the grid.

27:26.820 --> 27:30.840
We unpacked how smartphones use equivalence classes to guess our words.

27:30.840 --> 27:35.340
And how we are constantly battling those invisible authors for control of our own vocabulary.

27:35.580 --> 27:36.520
We covered a lot of ground.

27:36.880 --> 27:41.600
Based on everything Sliction lays out about substrate independence and this idea of remapping,

27:42.040 --> 27:44.040
I know you have a thought you wanted to leave us with.

27:44.180 --> 27:44.760
I do, yeah.

27:45.220 --> 27:47.140
Think about the trajectory of interfaces.

27:48.100 --> 27:55.300
What if the ultimate future of technology isn't about humans constantly being forced to learn how to use new, rigid hardware?

27:55.560 --> 27:55.800
Okay.

27:55.800 --> 28:01.400
What if it's about hardware that instantly contorts to fit the knowledge our bodies already possess?

28:02.160 --> 28:07.880
Imagine walking up to a completely alien machine, a computer interface you have never seen before in your life.

28:08.460 --> 28:15.400
You place your hands on a flat, featureless surface, and instantly you are able to communicate with it at 80 words a minute.

28:15.660 --> 28:16.220
Just immediately.

28:16.220 --> 28:23.960
Right. Not because you spent weeks learning its specific layout, but because the machine recognizes the unique motor grammar of your hands.

28:24.280 --> 28:26.800
The interface of the future might not have keys at all.

28:27.040 --> 28:30.360
It might just be a mirror for the language your fingers have already learned to speak.

28:30.860 --> 28:32.440
A mirror for the fingers.

28:32.440 --> 28:40.200
So the next time you sit down at your desk and place your hands on that familiar plastic grid, don't just look at the letters printed on the keys.

28:40.760 --> 28:41.800
Look down at your hands.

28:42.180 --> 28:42.900
Watch them work.

28:43.320 --> 28:49.220
And recognize the complex, embodied language they are effortlessly performing, completely on their own.

28:49.320 --> 28:50.460
You aren't just pushing buttons.

28:50.820 --> 28:53.920
You're speaking a language that doesn't even need a keyboard to exist.

28:53.920 --> 29:23.900
You're speaking a language that doesn't even need a keyboard to exist.

